module j3.net.tcp
    Blocking TCP: `dial` a host and port, then `read` / `writeAll` / `close` the `Conn`; `listen` and
    `accept` for a local server. Every call can block up to the timeout, so run network code on a
    `j3.thread` worker (`j3c doc recipes http`). TLS on top is `j3.net.tls`, HTTP is `j3.net.http`.
    Linux and Android; on macOS and Windows `dial` returns `TcpError.Fail` for now.

enum TcpError
    Why a TCP call failed.
    Resolve
        The host name did not resolve.
    Refused
        The host answered but nothing listens on that port.
    Timeout
        No answer within the timeout.
    Fail
        Any other OS failure.

const TIMEOUT: int = 0 - 3
    `Conn.read` result: no data within the timeout. Same value as `netsys.TIMEOUT`.

const FAIL: int = 0 - 2
    `Conn.read` result: the connection failed. Same value as `netsys.FAIL`.

struct Conn
    One open connection. Close it once with `close`.
    fd: int = -1
        The socket.
    fn Conn.read(this: &Conn, buf: []u8): int
        Read up to `buf.len` bytes: the count, 0 when the peer closed, `TIMEOUT`, or `FAIL`.
    fn Conn.writeAll(this: &Conn, data: []u8): bool
        Write all of `data`. False when the connection failed or timed out.
    fn Conn.setTimeout(this: &Conn, ms: int)
        Make each later `read` / `writeAll` give up after `ms` (0 waits forever).
    fn Conn.close(this: &Conn)
        Close the connection. Safe to call twice.

fn dial(host: string, port: int, timeoutMs: int, arena: &Arena): Result<Conn, TcpError>
    Connect to `host:port`, trying each resolved address in turn, each for up to `timeoutMs`.
    The same timeout then applies to every `read` and `writeAll`.
struct Listener
    A listening socket. `accept` takes the next connection.
    fd: int = -1
        The socket.
    port: int = 0
        The port it listens on (the real one when `listen` was given 0).
    fn Listener.accept(this: &Listener): Result<Conn, TcpError>
        Wait for the next connection.
    fn Listener.close(this: &Listener)
        Stop listening.

fn listen(port: int): Result<Listener, TcpError>
    Listen on `127.0.0.1:port` (`0` picks a free port; read it from `Listener.port`).
    Loopback only: this is for local tools and tests. `listenOn` takes an interface address.
fn listenOn(addr: string, port: int): Result<Listener, TcpError>
    Listen on the IPv4 address `addr` (`"0.0.0.0"` is every interface, `"100.64.1.2"` one of them)
    at `port` (`0` picks a free port). `Err(TcpError.Resolve)` when `addr` is not a dotted IPv4
    address, `Err(TcpError.Fail)` when the port is taken or the address is not on this machine.
